{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/675226780bb9627524f4ab03691e84e312300f398f352abc3b698c1fcdd83490.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-1$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=0$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=1$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=1=f(-1).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-1$ since ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=0=f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(1,3),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=2,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(2)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-161866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-161845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-161857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/aa872c37fc7912e4c246c80c391ee37ae57f23f6c1010681c367ce2ec6d901c2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.88$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.5$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0.5,1.5),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-0.88=f(0),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is also false. Notice that $y=2.5=f(3).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=3$ since ${f}^{′}(3)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-161865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/540e1fac13ed45d6db5c857678a7ccf410b3834213adb2b984cb6f942d4dba2f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=2=f(-2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-2$ since ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=4=f(0),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=0.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=0,$ for example, $(-0.1,0.1),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=0,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(0)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-161858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-161858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-(-2v)\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=0$ defines the point $(0,0)$ in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-160460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=\\pm v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm 2u.$"] ["text" "The only critical point is $(0,0).$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The only critical point is $(0,0).$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-160473" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160473" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=6.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $2{v}^{2}-3{u}^{2}=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=6u.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the parabola $2v=3{u}^{2}.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160473" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{uv}$ and $y(u,v)=u+2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v{e}^{uv} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u{e}^{uv} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot 2-u{e}^{uv}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(2v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{uv}(2v-u) & =0 \\\\ 2v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe equation $u=2v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-81274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $uv=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=-2v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=-2u.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=2u.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=u{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+2v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 2uv \\\\ 2u & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{2}\\cdot 2-2uv\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v & =0 \\\\ v(2v-4{u}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v(2v-4{u}^{2})=0$ defines the line $v=0,$ i.e., the $u$-axis, and the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}$ in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-160477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the hyperbola ${v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=-2v$ and $u=v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}.$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=uv$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ v & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-81169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x-y$ and $v(x,y)=x+2y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-81333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-6y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+-y$ and $v(x,y)=x+3y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-160563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-x+7y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=-x+7y$ and $v(x,y)=3x-y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 3-7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-160581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{10}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-160581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{10}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-81305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4v"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2v}"] ["math" "2u"] ["math" "4v"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4v}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4v}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-4y \\\\ 2x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=5x-4y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+3y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =5 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7e67977061ddfaed8f67871389365806e06be0929114d14a4ff92d2f2e1ca88"} {"question" "q-81370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "23"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{23}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "q-81370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{23}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/state.json" "file_sha256" "feea559162f96b8abeaf7d932a4925684431480dd033a64ba920d4ef84b7eb1e"} {"question" "e-12416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/6e97c59a7c736ec70142bc402566385fc2cd9c6aa78e304d70cefab1027078c1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=0$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem?\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\n- $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=0$\n\n- The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y=-1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/example-12416.json" "file_sha256" "da52b59f7fd98f55a4f15da0c4e3a59a9d60db635dc5cfc2828d064aa62f50e4"} {"question" "e-12416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x = -3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y = 0 = f(0).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 0$ since $f'(0) = 0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y = -1.1 = f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(0,4),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x = 2,$ i.e., $f'(2) \\neq 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = -1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/example-12416.json" "file_sha256" "da52b59f7fd98f55a4f15da0c4e3a59a9d60db635dc5cfc2828d064aa62f50e4"} {"question" "e-7127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/4a20bc6cf78708f70fb1a696b9dc5971feef974df6429fa4169f82881c6d22e0.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/example-7127.json" "file_sha256" "58cba56cf01830d88b619905cd31debd31ee801ec3e5bd2f2ce02cc00fe83765"} {"question" "e-7127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = u^2-v^2$ and $y(u,v) = u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ \\,\\,1\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2(u+v) & =0 \\\\ u & =-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$  in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/example-7127.json" "file_sha256" "58cba56cf01830d88b619905cd31debd31ee801ec3e5bd2f2ce02cc00fe83765"} {"question" "e-11157" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/2614cbd879ea1014caf69d1239b69e81ebefcc76b8946962092cb83cc96dc987.png)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/example-11157.json" "file_sha256" "ca4a36234fa5553499812f8f86700441d1e90136b05a6b375da6f468ef3474a7"} {"question" "e-11157" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/149438cd70059230993ca93a2122158f71edb665b3948cbd4ff3a9c038d7e48f.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x-y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/example-11157.json" "file_sha256" "ca4a36234fa5553499812f8f86700441d1e90136b05a6b375da6f468ef3474a7"} {"question" "q-81169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "9a88dfac29af584d4a4efc262a6c5c959e181db7a209bde191aec2f6d10f1d4a" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "For T(u,v)=(uv,u+v), the derivative matrix has rows (v,u) 