84d4a4efc262a6c5c959e181db7a209bde191aec2f6d10f1d4a" "rationale" "For T(u,v)=(uv,u+v), the derivative matrix has rows (v,u) and (1,1), so its determinant is v-u. It is singular exactly when v=u. Thus all critical points lie on that line, original option b. The authentic worked solution confirms the derivation, and saved grading reports Correct. The stored key states the same result with different spacing and punctuation serialization."}] "usage" {"e-7127" [] "e-11157" [] "e-12416" [] "q-81305" [] "q-81169" []} "basis" 1662 "retractions" [[17592186198928 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u + v\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186198928 :question/answer-fields 17592186197803] [17592186198929 :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$. The inverse transformation $T^{-1}(x, y) → (u(x, y), v(x, y))$ is given by\n\n$T^{-1}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v$."] [17592186197970 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$, whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=0$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3$. Which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4149/Source/Images/af9f81048aa4f33fd0c1b0f79d35ca30.png)\n\n1. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n2. $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=0$\n3. The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y=-1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$"] [17592186197970 :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n> *If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R$, $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U$, then*\n> - *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a$,*\n> - *$f^{-1}$, the inverse of $f$, is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b$, and*\n> - *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by*\n> $\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x = -3$. So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y = 0 = f(0)$. However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 0$ since $f'(0) = 0$. So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y = -1.1 = f(2)$, and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 2$. Indeed,$f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2$, for example, $(0,4)$, andthe derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x = 2$, i.e., $f'(2) \\neq 0$.Therefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = -1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186198161 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186198161 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T$, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = 0\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx(u,v) = u^2-v^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(u,v) = u+v\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 2u & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|=-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(u + v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= 0 \\\\\n2(u + v) &= 0 \\\\\nu &= -v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$ in the $uv$-plane."] [17592186197940 :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$. The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v$."] [17592186197940 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}$. So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)}$, the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}$.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu(x,y) = e^{x+y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nv(x,y) = e^{x-y}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} &= e^{x + y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y}|= e^{x + y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x}|= e^{x - y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}|=-e^{x - y}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{x + y} & e^{x + y} \\\\ e^{x - y} & -e^{x - y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{x + y} \\cdot (-e^{x - y}) - e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} \\\\\n&=-e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} - e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot e^{x + y} \\cdot e^{x - y} \\\\\n&=-2uv\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= ((∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)))^{-1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2uv}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186197804 17592186197805 17592186197806 17592186197807 17592186197808 17592186197810 17592186197811 17592186197812 17592186197813 17592186197814] "immutable_fields" [[17592186197803 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197803 :answer-field/id] [17592186197803 :answer-field/key] [17592186197803 :answer-field/type] [17592186197809 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197809 :answer-field/id] [17592186197809 :answer-field/key] [17592186197809 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
